<pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.14" change_id="I89968d51fbe2a98305bff4a01e0e0bc4ac9946d1" commit="1a907d1c6bf3098098353917bf296ad39f436e0c" commit_message="FROMLIST: common: DMA-mapping: add DMA_ATTR_NOHUGEPAGE attribute  This patch adds the DMA_ATTR_NOHUGEPAGE attribute to the DMA-mapping subsystem.  This attribute can be used as a hint to the DMA-mapping subsystem that it's likely not worth it to try to allocation large pages behind the scenes.  Large pages are likely to make an IOMMU TLB work more efficiently but may not be worth it.  See the Documentation contained in this patch for more details about this attribute and when to use it.  Note that the name of the hint (DMA_ATTR_NOHUGEPAGE) is based on the name MADV_NOHUGEPAGE, which has the same meaning.  If we have expected users, we could also add MADV_HUGEPAGE which has the opposite meaning of this hint.  BUG=chromium:570532 TEST=Memory pressure + cat videos is even smoother!  Change-Id: I89968d51fbe2a98305bff4a01e0e0bc4ac9946d1 Signed-off-by: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Acked-by: Marek Szyprowski &lt;m.szyprowski@samsung.com&gt; (am from https://patchwork.kernel.org/patch/7981221/) Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/320496 Reviewed-by: Tomasz Figa &lt;tfiga@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="320496" owner_email="dianders@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="4" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/96/320496/4" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.14" change_id="I7d1c747227afb777b3a7be99552538698ca68799" commit="3ca1a6c107511ca44ad666734c893a224642a050" commit_message="FROMLIST: ARM: dma-mapping: Use DMA_ATTR_NOHUGEPAGE hint to optimize allocation  If we know that TLB efficiency will not be an issue when memory is accessed then it's not terribly important to allocate big chunks of memory.  The whole point of allocating the big chunks was that it would make TLB usage efficient.  As Marek Szyprowski indicated:     Please note that mapping memory with larger pages significantly     improves performance, especially when IOMMU has a little TLB     cache. This can be easily observed when multimedia devices do     processing of RGB data with 90/270 degree rotation Image rotation is distinctly an operation that needs to bounce around through memory, so it makes sense that TLB efficiency is important there.  Video decoding, on the other hand, is a fairly sequential operation. During video decoding it's not expected that we'll be jumping all over memory.  Decoding video is also pretty heavy and the TLB misses aren't a huge deal.  Presumably most HW video acceleration users of dma-mapping will not care about huge pages and will set DMA_ATTR_NOHUGEPAGE.  Allocating big chunks of memory is quite expensive, especially if we're doing it repeadly and memory is full.  In one (out of tree) usage model it is common that arm_iommu_alloc_attrs() is called 16 times in a row, each one trying to allocate 4 MB of memory.  This is called whenever the system encounters a new video, which could easily happen while the memory system is stressed out.  In fact, on certain social media websites that auto-play video and have infinite scrolling, it's quite common to see not just one of these 16x4MB allocations but 2 or 3 right after another.  Asking the system even to do a small amount of extra work to give us big chunks in this case is just not a good use of time.  Allocating big chunks of memory is also expensive indirectly.  Even if we ask the system not to do ANY extra work to allocate _our_ memory, we're still potentially eating up all big chunks in the system. Presumably there are other users in the system that aren't quite as flexible and that actually need these big chunks.  By eating all the big chunks we're causing extra work for the rest of the system.  We also may start making other memory allocations fail.  While the system may be robust to such failures (as is the case with dwc2 USB trying to allocate buffers for Ethernet data and with WiFi trying to allocate buffers for WiFi data), it is yet another big performance hit.  BUG=chromium:570532 TEST=Memory pressure + cat videos is even smoother!  Change-Id: I7d1c747227afb777b3a7be99552538698ca68799 Signed-off-by: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Acked-by: Marek Szyprowski &lt;m.szyprowski@samsung.com&gt; (am from https://patchwork.kernel.org/patch/7981231/) Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/320497 Reviewed-by: Tomasz Figa &lt;tfiga@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="320497" owner_email="dianders@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="4" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/97/320497/4" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="chromeos-3.14" change_id="Idedce8ea07d48d320d8f64b4cb68282292149066" commit="5396ab6d4ce2a2580e0593ffdaaf104403afde8c" commit_message="CHROMIUM: rk3288-vpu: Set DMA_ATTR_NOHUGEPAGE  We do video allocation all the time and we need it to be fast.  Plus TLB efficiency isn't terribly important for video.  That means we want to set DMA_ATTR_NOHUGEPAGE.  See also the previous change (&quot;ARM: dma-mapping: Use DMA_ATTR_NOHUGEPAGE hint to optimize allocation&quot;).  BUG=chromium:570532 TEST=Memory pressure + cat videos is even smoother!  Change-Id: Idedce8ea07d48d320d8f64b4cb68282292149066 Signed-off-by: Douglas Anderson &lt;dianders@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/320498 Reviewed-by: Tomasz Figa &lt;tfiga@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="320498" owner_email="dianders@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="4" project="chromiumos/third_party/kernel" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/kernel" ref="refs/changes/98/320498/4" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I42ae09cba706e470d06471f6cc309bdcceae688c" commit="a2af002b6de6f2a4bd182ca1bd01da7e941ba882" commit_message="system: Move MSR to thermal_slope test.  The MSR (Model Specific Register) module is currently only used to retrieve energy status for thermal_slope test.  It is possible to provide such information in dut.status module but (1) It's very specific to x86 and no different per-board implementations (2) We don't need anything else from it yet, so a simpler solution is to just move MSRSnapshot into thermal slope test itself. If we need to use MSR in future we can move it to dut.status with right usage.  BUG=chromium:557573 TEST=make test  Change-Id: I42ae09cba706e470d06471f6cc309bdcceae688c Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/320525 Commit-Ready: Hung-Te Lin &lt;hungte@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Hung-Te Lin &lt;hungte@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Wei-Han Chen &lt;stimim@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="320525" owner_email="hungte@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="3" project="chromiumos/platform/factory" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/factory" ref="refs/changes/25/320525/3" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I967fa5a18e534ffb9a9bbac850544eeecde490c4" commit="d23d780213fb23b502e40c205b5795d08f7cbcf1" commit_message="dut: Add DUT-aware &quot;bluetooth&quot; component.  Move the bluetooth module from system to DUT folder. The bluetooth module is currently using DBus so it can't run remotely.  The API was also changed to remove the argument (host_mac) in constructor because it's only used in GetFirstAdapter and GetAdapters, which can be made as the argument for those two functions (and is more flexible if a test wants to use multiple bluetooth host adapters).  BUG=chromium:557573 TEST=make test  Change-Id: I967fa5a18e534ffb9a9bbac850544eeecde490c4 Reviewed-on